Use mass points: place masses $m_A,m_B,m_C$ at the vertices so that $D$ is the balance point of $A,B$ and $E$ the balance point of $B,C$. This requires $m_A\cdot AD=m_B\cdot DB$, i.e.\ $m_A:m_B=5:3$, and $m_C\cdot CE=m_B\cdot EB$, i.e.\ $m_C:m_B=3:8$. In integers, $m_A=40$, $m_B=24$, $m_C=9$ (blue in the sketch).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(8,0),C=(2.5,5.5);
pair D=A+3/8*(B-A), Ep=C+8/11*(B-C);
pair G=extension(A,Ep,C,D), F=extension(B,G,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ep);draw(C--D);draw(B--F);
dot(D);dot(Ep);dot(F);dot(G);
label("$A$",A,dir(160));label("$B$",B,dir(20));label("$C$",C,N);label("$D$",D,S);label("$E$",Ep,NE);label("$F$",F,NW);label("$G$",G,dir(55));
label("$40$",A+(0,-0.75),blue);label("$24$",B+(0,-0.75),blue);label("$9$",C+(0.75,0.1),blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The centre of mass of the three masses lies on both cevians $AE$ and $CD$, so it is $G$; it also lies on the segment from $B$ to $F$, the balance point of $A$ and $C$, which carries mass $m_A+m_C=49$. Balancing on $BF$,
\[
\dfrac{FG}{GB}=\dfrac{m_B}{m_F}=\dfrac{24}{49}.
\]
So $FG:GB=\boxed{24:49}$.

(Check with Ceva's theorem: $\dfrac{AD}{DB}\cdot\dfrac{BE}{EC}\cdot\dfrac{CF}{FA}=1$ gives $\dfrac{CF}{FA}=\dfrac{5}{3}\cdot\dfrac{8}{3}=\dfrac{40}{9}=\dfrac{m_A}{m_C}$, as it should.)

The quadrilateral $OYPZ$ has right angles at $O$, $Y$ and $Z$, so it is a rectangle (if $P$ is an endpoint of a diameter it degenerates, and the statement below still holds).

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real R=8; pair O=(0,0),P=R*dir(55),Y=(P.x,0),Z=(0,P.y);
draw(circle(O,R),linewidth(0.9));
draw((-R,0)--(R,0));draw((0,-R)--(0,R));
draw(P--Y);draw(P--Z);draw(Y--Z,blue+linewidth(1));draw(O--P,dashed);
draw(Y+(0,0.8)--Y+(-0.8,0.8)--Y+(-0.8,0));draw(Z+(0.8,0)--Z+(0.8,-0.8)--Z+(0,-0.8));
dot(O);dot(P);dot(Y);dot(Z);
label("$A$",(-R,0),W);label("$B$",(R,0),E);label("$C$",(0,R),N);label("$D$",(0,-R),S);
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$P$",P,NE);label("$Y$",Y,S);label("$Z$",Z,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so $YZ=OP=8$, the radius, wherever $P$ is on the circle. The answer is $\boxed{\mathrm{(B)}\ 8}$.

The reflection is $A'(4,2)$, $B'(-1,2)$, $C'(1,5)$. Both triangles have their bases on the line $y=2$, and the overlap lies above the segment from $B'(-1,2)$ to $B(1,2)$, bounded by side $BC$ of the first triangle and side $B'C'$ of the second.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-4,2),B=(1,2),C=(-1,5),A1=(4,2),B1=(-1,2),C1=(1,5),X=(0,3.5);
draw((-5,0)--(5,0),gray,Arrow(5));draw((0,-0.5)--(0,6.2),gray,Arrow(5));
fill(B1--B--X--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A1--B1--C1--cycle,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(A1);dot(B1);dot(C1);dot(X);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,S);label("$C$",C,N);
label("$A'$",A1,SE,blue);label("$B'$",B1,S,blue);label("$C'$",C1,N,blue);
label("$X$",X,dir(15));label("$x$",(5,0),S);label("$y$",(0,6.2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Line $BC$ has slope $\dfrac{5-2}{-1-1}=-\dfrac{3}{2}$, so it is $y=2-\dfrac{3}{2}(x-1)$; by symmetry $B'C'$ is $y=2+\dfrac{3}{2}(x+1)$. They meet on the $y$-axis at $X=(0,3.5)$. The other sides $AC$ and $A'C'$ lie outside this region (for example $AC$ is at $x\le-1$). So the overlap is triangle $B'BX$ with base $2$ and height $1.5$, of area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot2\cdot1.5=\boxed{1.5}$.

The quadrilateral (with its vertices in cyclic order) has a side of the octagon exactly when two chosen vertices are adjacent on the octagon. So we need $4$ of the $8$ vertices of a cycle with no two adjacent. Between consecutive chosen vertices there must be at least one unchosen vertex, and there are only $8-4=4$ unchosen ones, so exactly one unchosen vertex lies in each gap: the chosen vertices alternate. That gives $2$ sets, the two inscribed squares (one in blue, the other gray dashed):

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair[] V;for(int i=0;i<8;++i)V[i]=dir(22.5+45*i);
draw(V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--V[6]--V[7]--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(V[0]--V[2]--V[4]--V[6]--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));draw(V[1]--V[3]--V[5]--V[7]--cycle,gray+dashed);
for(int i=0;i<8;++i)dot(V[i]);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The probability is $\dfrac{2}{\dbinom84}=\dfrac{2}{70}=\boxed{\dfrac{1}{35}}$.

Let $O$ be the centre of the cube. The half-turn (point reflection) about $O$ maps the cube to itself and swaps opposite vertices and opposite edges: $E\leftrightarrow C$, and edge $FB\leftrightarrow$ edge $HD$ with their midpoints $J\leftrightarrow I$. So it maps the plane $EJCI$ to itself. (The plane does pass through $O$, the midpoint of $EC$.)

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real a=4; pair k=(0.42,0.55);
pair A=(0,0),B=(a,0),C=(a,0)+a*k,D=a*k;
pair Ep=A+(0,a),F=B+(0,a),G=C+(0,a),H=D+(0,a);
pair J=(F+B)/2,I=(H+D)/2;
fill(Ep--J--C--I--cycle,lightgray+opacity(0.6));
draw(A--B--F--Ep--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(Ep--H--G--F,linewidth(0.9));draw(B--C--G,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,dashed+gray);draw(D--C,dashed+gray);draw(D--H,dashed+gray);
draw(Ep--J--C,blue+linewidth(0.9));draw(C--I--Ep,blue+dashed);
dot(J);dot(I);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,E);label("$D$",D,dir(160));
label("$E$",Ep,W);label("$F$",F,dir(-10));label("$G$",G,NE);label("$H$",H,NW);
label("$J$",J,dir(-50));label("$I$",I,1.5*dir(35));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The half-turn also swaps the two sides of this plane: for example $G$, on the same side as $F$ and $H$, goes to $A$ on the other side. Hence the two pieces are congruent, and the piece $EFGHICJ$ has $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{2}}$ of the cube's volume. (Its cross-section $EJCI$ is a rhombus.)

Let the side be $s$ and use coordinates $A=(0,0)$, $B=(s,0)$, $C=(s,s)$, $D=(0,s)$, $E=\left(\dfrac {s}{2},s\right)$. Triangles $FAB$ and $FCE$ are similar (since $AB\parallel CE$) with ratio $AB:CE=2:1$, so $F$ divides $AC$ in the ratio $2:1$: $F=\left(\dfrac{2s}{3},\dfrac{2s}{3}\right)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=6; pair A=(0,0),B=(s,0),C=(s,s),D=(0,s),Ep=(s/2,s),F=(2s/3,2s/3);
fill(A--F--Ep--D--cycle,lightgray);fill(B--F--C--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw(A--C);draw(B--Ep);
dot(Ep);dot(F);
label("$A$",A,SW);label("$B$",B,SE);label("$C$",C,NE);label("$D$",D,NW);label("$E$",Ep,N);label("$F$",F,dir(-20));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangle $CEF$ has base $CE=\dfrac {s}{2}$ and height $s-\dfrac{2s}{3}=\dfrac {s}{3}$, so area $\dfrac{s^2}{12}$; hence $[AFED]=[ACD]-[CEF]=\dfrac{s^2}{2}-\dfrac{s^2}{12}=\dfrac{5s^2}{12}=45$, so $s^2=108$. Triangle $BFC$ has base $BC=s$ and height $s-\dfrac{2s}{3}=\dfrac {s}{3}$ (the horizontal distance from $F$ to $BC$), so
\[
[BFC]=\dfrac{s^2}{6}=\boxed{18}.
\]

Let the sides be $a\le b\le c$ with $a+b+c=30$. The triangle inequality reduces to $a+b>c$, i.e.\ $30-c>c$, so $c\le14$; and $c\ge\dfrac{30}{3}=10$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,0),B=(12,0);real a=8,c=10;real x=(12^2+c^2-a^2)/(2*12);pair C=(x,sqrt(c^2-x^2));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$c$",(A+B)/2,S);label("$b$",(A+C)/2,NW);label("$a$",(B+C)/2,NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

For each $c$, the pairs $a\le b\le c$ with $a+b=30-c$ have $b$ running from $\left\lceil\dfrac{30-c}{2}\right\rceil$ to $c$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
c&10&11&12&13&14\\\hline
a+b&20&19&18&17&16\\
b&10&10,11&9..12&9..13&8..14\\
\text{count}&1&2&4&5&7
\end{array}
\]
The total is $1+2+4+5+7=\boxed{19}$. (A closed form: for even perimeter $n$ the count is the integer nearest to $\dfrac{n^2}{48}$; here $\dfrac{900}{48}=18.75$, giving $19$.)

Since $TU\parallel BC$, triangle $ATU$ is similar to triangle $ABC$ with ratio $k=\dfrac{8-t}{8}$, the ratio of their heights from $A$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(0,8),B=(2,0),C=(8,0),T=(1.5,2),U=(6,2);
draw((-1,0)--(9.5,0),gray,Arrow(5));draw((0,-0.8)--(0,9.3),gray,Arrow(5));
fill(A--T--U--cycle,lightgray);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));draw((-0.6,2)--(9,2),blue);
dot(T);dot(U);
label("$A(0,8)$",A,E);label("$B(2,0)$",B,S);label("$C(8,0)$",C,S);label("$T$",T,1.5*dir(145));label("$U$",U,NE);label("$y=t$",(9,2),N,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Triangle $ABC$ has base $BC=6$ and height $8$, so area $24$. Areas scale by $k^2$: $24k^2=13.5$ gives $k^2=\dfrac{9}{16}$, $k=\dfrac{3}{4}$, so $8-t=6$ and $t=\boxed{2}$. (Then $T=(1.5,2)$, $U=(6,2)$, $TU=4.5$, and $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot4.5\cdot6=13.5$.)

The moves have lengths $1,2,\dots,m$ with total $1+2+\dots+m=28$, so $m=7$. A path is determined by the set of move lengths that go in the $x$-direction, which must sum to $14$ (the rest then also sum to $14$). Count subsets of $\{1,\dots,7\}$ with sum $14$, sorted by size:
\begin{itemize}
\item three elements: $\{7,6,1\},\{7,5,2\},\{7,4,3\},\{6,5,3\}$;
\item four elements: their complements $\{2,3,4,5\},\{1,3,4,6\},\{1,2,5,6\},\{1,2,4,7\}$;
\item two or fewer elements give at most $7+6=13$, and five or more are complements of those.
\end{itemize}
So there are $\boxed{8}$ paths.

Both lines pass through the origin. Their unit direction vectors in the first quadrant are $\left(\dfrac{3}{5},\dfrac{4}{5}\right)$ and $\left(\dfrac{5}{13},\dfrac{12}{13}\right)$, and the angle between these is acute (their dot product $\dfrac{63}{65}$ is positive). The sum of two unit vectors lies along the bisector of the angle between them (the parallelogram they span is a rhombus):
\[
\left(\dfrac{3}{5}+\dfrac{5}{13},\ \dfrac{4}{5}+\dfrac{12}{13}\right)=\left(\dfrac{64}{65},\dfrac{112}{65}\right).
\]

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair u=(3,4)/5,v=(5,12)/13,w=unit(u+v);
draw((-0.3,0)--(5,0),gray,Arrow(5));draw((0,-0.3)--(0,5.4),gray,Arrow(5));
draw(O--5.2*u,linewidth(0.9));draw(O--4.9*v,linewidth(0.9));draw(O--5.3*w,blue+dashed);
draw(O--u,red+linewidth(1),Arrow(4));draw(O--v,red+linewidth(1),Arrow(4));draw(u--u+v--v,red+dotted);
label("$y=\dfrac{4}{3}x$",5.2*u,E);label("$y=\dfrac{12}{5}x$",4.9*v,N);label("$y=\dfrac{7}{4}x$",5.3*w,NE,blue);
label("$x$",(5,0),S);label("$y$",(0,5.4),W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The bisector has slope $\dfrac{112}{64}=\boxed{\dfrac{7}{4}}$. (Check: the tangent of the angle between lines of slopes $m_1,m_2$ is $\left|\dfrac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\right|$; for $\dfrac{7}{4}$ with $\dfrac{4}{3}$ this is $\dfrac{\dfrac{5}{12}}{\dfrac{10}{3}}=\dfrac{1}{8}$, and for $\dfrac{7}{4}$ with $\dfrac{12}{5}$ it is $\dfrac{\dfrac{13}{20}}{\dfrac{26}{5}}=\dfrac{1}{8}$.)
